  "why": "The statement asks only whether infinitely many n make n·2ⁿ+1 prime. Each individual classical Cullen prime and the 2015 search range is a finite check, so those are instances. Every post-snapshot event is about a different question: primes of the form n·bⁿ+1 with b ≠ 2, repunit intersections, or membership in Padovan/Perrin sequences. All of those are variants. Hooley's density-zero theorem was the closest call. It does limit how many Cullen primes there can be, but it says nothing about whether there are infinitely many, so it neither settles nor bounds the question as asked. I scored it variant rather than bound, and since it predates the snapshot and is not resolved, the choice does not change the outcome. Caldwell's heuristic is not a result, so it is none. No AI system is credited anywhere.",
